  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n1\\. Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n2\\. Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n3\\. Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the second branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\ng(-1)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $g(x)$ is defined at $x=-1.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}(\\frac{x}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(\\frac{x}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}g(x)={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}g(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $g(x)$ at $x=-1$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne g(-1).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $g(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
